<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/110816878%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Dur-Šarrukin Archive 1";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/508774533%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/785467779%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/874458%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "Three tiers of niches were found in Room H 5 (on the northwestern side of Forecourt I) of the Nabû temple at Dur-Šarrukin, the short-lived capital of the Assyrian king Sargon II (721–705 BC). Fragments of clay tablets and inscribed prisms in these niches suggest that a temple library was located here. The empty niches might be a result of the move of the capital to Nineveh under Sennacherib (704–681 BC) or of the use of perishable writing materials.";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/110816878> .

